  "verdict": "revise",
  "scores": {
    "novelty": {
      "score": 4,
      "rationale": "The combination of continuous URIEL+ inverse-distance sampling + percentile-anchored staged curriculum + precisely formulated multi-source DPO for non-English pivots is genuinely novel; no competing paper was found that combines all three for agglutinative low-resource targets. The closest competitor (arXiv:2410.07809) uses discrete k-means cluster selection on legacy 289-dim URIEL with zero Kazakh/Mongolian/Uyghur evaluation, and TWMP's differentiation is thorough and accurate."
    },
    "feasibility": {
      "score": 3,
      "rationale": "Manual (120 H100h) and HIL (186 H100h) modes are within the ≤200 H100h budget and technically well-grounded; however, the 10-epoch QLoRA curriculum over Qwen3.5-9B repeated across 3 target languages + baselines + ablations is compute-intensive, and the paper correctly identifies ACL 2027 (August 2026 ARR) as the realistic primary target—the 16-day EMNLP 2026 window remains aspirational unless infrastructure is fully pre-built."
    },
    "clarity": {
      "score": 4,
      "rationale": "Substantially improved over Rounds 1–2: the DPO formulation is now precisely stated for the multi-source case, curriculum thresholds are anchored to empirical URIEL+ distance percentiles (D_25, D_60) with concrete epoch ranges (1–3, 4–6, 7–10), prerequisite probe threshold (>5% in K=8 samples) is specified, and all Aya pivot language counts with a 5,000-instance floor are documented."
    },
    "impact": {
      "score": 3,
      "rationale": "Addressing Kazakh, Mongolian, and Uyghur—languages where even GPT-4 performs near-randomly—via a principled generalizable method tied to three ACL 2025 benchmarks is meaningful; the held-out Swahili target tests cross-family generalization, but the evaluation is still limited to three primary targets and impact beyond the Turkic–Mongolic cluster remains undemonstrated."
    }
  },
  "issues": [
    "MODERATE – Sherkala-Chat (arXiv:2503.01493, March 2025) is a directly relevant uncited work: it instruction-tunes Llama-3.1-8B for Kazakh on 45.3B tokens of Kazakh, English, Russian, and Turkish data, demonstrating that Turkish as an auxiliary training language improves Kazakh performance—the empirical core insight that motivates TWMP's pivot selection. Not citing it leaves a gap reviewers will notice; TWMP should add it as related work and contrast it: Sherkala-Chat uses ad-hoc four-language mixing with full fine-tuning, whereas TWMP provides a principled, generalizable, LoRA-based method parameterized by continuous URIEL+ distances applicable across any agglutinative target.",
    "MODERATE – The DPO preference pair construction specifies 'highest-scoring chosen' and 'lowest-scoring rejected,' where 'scoring' is implicitly language identification confidence from fastText. This means a grammatically garbled Kazakh response with high fastText confidence could be chosen as the preferred response over a coherent Turkish response. No quality filter (perplexity, length heuristic, or instruction-following score) is applied to the chosen pool. Reviewers will ask whether the DPO training signal is noisy precisely for the close-pivot cases where K=8 Kazakh responses are plentiful—yet quality varies most—and for distant pairs where only 1 of 8 samples passes the language filter, leaving little variance to select from. The paper should either add a quality filter (e.g., exclude responses shorter than 20 tokens or with perplexity above a threshold under the base model) or explicitly acknowledge and ablate this as a limitation.",
    "MINOR – Qwen3.5-9B release date: the paper states 'released February 2026' but available information indicates the Qwen3.5 small series (0.8B/2B/4B/9B) became available March 2, 2026—the February date applies to the larger Qwen3.5 medium models (35B-A3B, 27B). This should be corrected to March 2026.",
    "MINOR – The URIEL+ repository URL cited in the approach section ('github.com/Masonshipton25/URIELPlus') does not match the official ACL Anthology-linked repository ('github.com/LeeLanguageLab/URIELPlus'). The URL should match the official publication's repository or the PyPI package ('urielplus') should be referenced instead.",
    "MINOR – The Kyrgyz Aya instance count ('~4.1M instances in the Aya collection language split') could not be independently verified against HuggingFace statistics. Given Kyrgyz is much lower-resource than Turkish, this count depends entirely on machine-translated data volume and should be verified and cited to the HuggingFace dataset page or the Aya Collection paper's per-language appendix before submission."
  ],
  "feedback": "The generator has addressed all Round 2 issues competently: the multi-source DPO formulation is now precisely specified (chosen = source-language instruction + model-generated target-language response; rejected = source-language instruction + model-generated source-language response), URIEL+ replaces legacy 289-dim features with the 'Less is More' 300-feature Laplacian subset, the staged curriculum has concrete D_25/D_60 percentile anchors and epoch ranges, DCO is cited and differentiated, Aya coverage is verified with a 5,000-instance floor, and auto_research mode is correctly flagged as exceeding the 200 H100h budget. Two moderate issues remain before this is submission-ready: (1) Add Sherkala-Chat (arXiv:2503.01493) to related work—it is a directly adjacent March 2025 paper training Llama-3.1 on Kazakh+Turkish+Russian and provides empirical motivation for TWMP's pivot selection logic. The contrast is easy: Sherkala-Chat is language-specific full fine-tuning with ad-hoc pivot choice; TWMP is generalizable LoRA-based training with principled URIEL+ weighting. (2) Address the DPO pair quality concern: define 'highest-scoring' explicitly as language ID confidence, acknowledge that this does not filter for response quality, and either add a minimal quality gate (e.g., minimum response length or base-model perplexity cap) or include it as a stated limitation with an ablation that shows the fastText-only selection is adequate in practice. Fix the three minor issues (Qwen3.5 release date, URIEL+ repo URL, Kyrgyz instance count verification) before submitting. ACL 2027 (August 3, 2026 ARR) remains the appropriate primary target; given these are all fixable within days, the paper is on track.",
  "reason": "All Round 2 blockers resolved; two moderate issues remain (missing Sherkala-Chat citation, unspecified DPO quality filter) and three minor factual errors prevent a clean pass at the 4.0 threshold."
}
